<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="algorithm_analysis_exercises">
  <title>Algorithm Analysis: Exercises</title>
    <p>
        Here are exercises to practice your understanding.
    </p>
  <exercises>
          <exercise><p>Give the Big O performance of the following code fragment:</p>

            <program language="kotlin" xml:id="big_o_nested_loop">
                for (i in 0 ..&lt; n) {
                    for (j in 0 ..&lt; n) {
                        val k = i + j
                    }
                }
            </program>
         </exercise>

        <exercise><p>Give the Big O performance of the following code fragment:</p>

        <program language="kotlin" xml:id="big_o_for_loop">
            for (i in 0 ..&lt; n) {
                val k = 2 * i
            }
            </program>
        </exercise>

        <exercise><p>Give the Big O performance of the following code fragment:</p>

            <program language="kotlin" xml:id="big_o_while_loop">
            var i = n
            while (i > 0) {
                val k = 2 * i
                i = i / 2
            }
            </program>
            </exercise>

        <exercise><p>Give the Big O performance of the following code fragment:</p>

   <program language="kotlin" xml:id="big_o_triple_nested_loop">
       var result = 0
       for (i in 0 ..&lt; n) {
          for (j in 0 ..&lt; n) {
             for (k in 0 ..&lt; n) {
                result = i + j + k
             }
          }
        }
            </program>
        </exercise>

        <exercise><p>Give the Big O performance of the following code fragment:</p>

            <program language="kotlin" xml:id="big_o_three_loops">
       var result = 0
       for (i in 0 ..&lt; n) {
          result = i + i
       }
       for (j in 0 ..&lt; n) {
          result = j + j
       }
       for (k in 0 ..&lt; n) {
          result = k + k
       }
          </program>
        </exercise>

        <exercise><p>Devise an experiment to verify that the Kotlin list <c>indexOf</c> operation is
        <m>O(n)</m>.</p></exercise>

    <exercise><p>Devise an experiment to verify that <c>get</c> and <c>put</c> are
        <m>O(1)</m> for Kotlin maps.</p></exercise>


    <exercise><p>Devise an experiment that compares the performance of the <c>remove</c>
   operation function on lists and maps.</p></exercise>


   <exercise><p>Given a list of numbers in random order, write an algorithm that works in <m>O(n\log(n))</m>
to find the <m>k\text{th}</m> smallest number in the list.</p></exercise>


    <exercise><p>Can you improve the algorithm from the previous problem to be linear? Explain.</p></exercise>

</exercises>
</section>
